  "body": "## What changed\n\n- **Two optional wire-machine keys** (`crates/post/src/machine/edm_dielectric.rs`, new):\n  - `edm.tank`: `submerged` or `non-submerged`.\n  - `edm.flushing`: `high` or `low`.\n\n  Unstated means the operator sets it at the control. An unknown word refuses the `.mch` file, the project's machine and the MCP patch, naming the key and the allowed words. Both keys are wire keys: a sinker that states one is refused.\n- **Set through the existing undoable machine mount**: the Machine window's wire section has two new combos, Tank and Flushing (`crates/ui/src/egui_app/machine_edm.rs`), and the MCP `edit_machine` merge patch takes the same keys. No new command or MCP tool was needed.\n- **`assets/posts/sodick-wire.cps`** writes the 1998 Mark 21 manual's T words: T84 / T85 for high / low flushing, and T94 / T89 for submersed / non-submersed machining. They go after each threading (T91, or the operator's M00) and before the C word, flushing first, in the order of the manual's Program Example 1 (T84, T94, C451). The header says what is programmed and what is still the operator's. The T94 line warns that no fill or drain word is written. An unknown word is also refused by name in the post, and nothing is cut.\n- **Unstated: posts as today.** The #617 golden `nc/sodick-wire/wire_slug_punch.nc` is byte-identical (pinned).\n- **`assets/posts/wire-iso.cps`** writes a header note when the tank or flushing is stated, because it has no word for either. A program that states neither is unchanged.\n- **Machine mirror** (`crates/post/src/post/machine.rs`): a wire's `edm` gains `tank` / `flushing` only when they are stated.\n- Docs: `assets/posts/AGENTS.md`, `assets/machines/AGENTS.md`, `site/content/guide/operations/operations-edm.md`. The decision record is `docs/decisions/1499-sodick-flushing.md`.\n\n## New golden: `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/sodick-wire/wire_slug_punch_flushed.nc`\n\nThis is the #617 punch on a machine stating `tank: submerged, flushing: high`, posted through the app's gate. Its diff against the #617 golden is pinned by `sodick_wire_dielectric::a_submerged_high_flush_punch_posts_its_golden_through_the_gate`:\n- The header line `(TANK FILL AND FLUSHING ARE NOT PROGRAMMED - SET THEM AT THE CONTROL)` is replaced by `(T94 SUBMERGED MACHINING AFTER THREADING - NO FILL OR DRAIN WORD IS WRITTEN - CHECK THE TANK IS FULL)` and `(T84 HIGH-PRESSURE FLUSHING AFTER THREADING)`.\n- `T91` (the wire is threaded at the start hole) is followed by `T84` (high pressure flushing on, manual T-code table), then `T94` (switch to submersed machining, manual T-code table), then `C110` (the condition, after both, in the Program Example 1 order).\n\nEvery other line is unchanged.\n\n## How tested\n\n- `crates/post/tests/sodick_dielectric.rs` (7 tests):\n  - every value's word, and that it comes after threading and before C;\n  - the manual's order;\n  - unstated posts as before;\n  - an M00 threading without AWT;\n  - an unknown word refused by name, with nothing cut;\n  - two sections each restating the words;\n  - the wire-iso note.\n- `crates/app/tests/sodick_wire_dielectric.rs` (3 tests): the golden, the unchanged #617 golden, and the round trip through the serde / `.mch` file with the garbled word refused.\n- `mcp_edit_machine::a_wire_tank_and_flushing_edit_is_one_undo_step_and_an_unknown_word_is_refused`: one undo step, unknown words refused with nothing changed, and a sinker refused.\n- `edm_dielectric` unit tests, and the UI test `machine_wire_edm::the_wire_machine_editor_states_tank_and_flushing`.\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` is green, and `cargo clippy -p camcraft-post -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- Not run on a Sodick control.\n- The relative order of T91 and T84 / T94 is this PR's reading (the decision file says why). The issue text says \"T94, T91, T84\", but the manual pages read here show T84 then T94 with no T91.\n- The fill / drain / pump / wire-drive T codes are not written.\n\nOverlap: #2120 (#1497) also edits `sodick-wire.cps`, in a different hunk (`onRapid`), so this PR is branched from main, not stacked. It does not overlap #1921.\n\nChangelog: A wire EDM machine can state its tank (submerged / non-submerged) and flushing (high / low); the Sodick wire post writes T94 / T89 and T84 / T85 after threading (#1499)\nEffort: high (not escalated)\n\nFixes #1499\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX\n",
